#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559692 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "563810e7-f31e-5609-8eea-711388584e00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559718 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "84661f42-becc-5c57-a165-fc16451678cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559728 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9973b090-3528-53c7-bd3d-78df1d7984eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559810 #edb/ref 1071 "Some customers in a pizzeria were asked about the type of pizza they prefer between spicy pizza and vegetarian pizza. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Prefers Spicy | Prefers  Vegetarian | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Children | $10$ | $22$ | $32$ |\n| Adults | $41$ | $27$ | $68$ |\n| Totals | $51$ | $49$ | $100$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen person is an adult or prefers spicy pizza (or both)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c991f61-1cd6-5a73-ae3c-f2cc1cf3d784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559810 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen person is an adult or prefers spicy pizza, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Adults\"** or the column **\"Prefers Spicy\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Adults\"** or the column **\"Prefers Spicy\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Prefers Spicy | Prefers Vegetarian | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Children | $10$ | $22$ | $32$ |\n| Adults | $41$ | $27$ | $68$ |\n| Totals | $51$ | $49$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{10+41+27}{100}=\\frac{78}{100}=0.78.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559817 #edb/ref 1071 "Roger surveyed $62$ Jefferson High School students. He asked them if they like biology and chemistry. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nEdison High School has $500$ students. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both high schools, determine the number of Edison High School students who like biology or chemistry but not both. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7775a984-c5ac-5540-8a1b-b2aae6fb0655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559817 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the number of students who like biology or chemistry but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Like chemistry\"** or the column **\" Like biology\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(62),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8+19}{62}=\\frac{27}{62}=0.435.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student likes biology or chemistry but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of Edison High School who like biology or chemistry but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the Edison High School $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =500\\cdot p \\\\  & =500\\cdot 0.435 \\\\  & =217.5 \\\\  & =218,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559833 #edb/ref 1071 "A randomly selected group of students from a high school were asked about their favorite sport between baseball, football, basketball, or soccer. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Baseball | Football | Basketball | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $19$ | $10$ | $12$ | $51$ |\n| Boys | $10$ | $13$ | $20$ | $6$ | $49$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $32$ | $30$ | $18$ | $100$ |\n\nLet $F$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a **\"Girl\"** whose favorite sport is **\"Football\"**, and $S$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a **\"Girl\"** whose favorite sport is **\"Soccer\"**. Estimate $P(F\\cup S).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9952b961-280f-5d1f-a0eb-bf2ca705ef14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559833 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $F\\cup S$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a girl whose favorite sport is football or a girl whose favorite sport is soccer. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $F\\cup S,$ we\n\n- add up the numbers in the columns **\"Football\"** or **\"Soccer\"** and the row **\"Girls\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the columns **\"Football\"** or **\"Soccer\"** and the row **\"Girls\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Baseball | Football | Basketball | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $19$ | $10$ | $12$ | $51$ |\n| Boys | $10$ | $13$ | $20$ | $6$ | $49$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $32$ | $30$ | $18$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(F\\cup S)=\\frac{19+12}{100}=\\frac{31}{100}=0.31.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559867 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559867 #edb/ref 1071 "A group of randomly selected customers from a certain insurance company was asked about the type of vehicle driven and whether they received a speeding ticket in the last year. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nThere are $220$ drivers in another insurance company. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both companies, estimate the number of drivers in the second insurance company that drive SUV or received a speeding ticket, but not both." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d65fd340-5d3c-5485-87ce-64ffa546bd48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559867 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected driver drives the SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Speeding ticket\"** or the column **\"SUV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(110),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{20+5}{110}=\\frac{5}{22}.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected driver drives an SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of drivers in the second company that drive SUVs or received a speeding ticket, but not both, we multiply the total number of drivers in the company $(220)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =220\\cdot p \\\\  & =220\\cdot \\frac{5}{22} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559873 #edb/ref 1071 "Dana surveys $72$ randomly chosen students at her school, asking them if they have gerbils or rabbits at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e30d37ae-3013-5226-8257-f893cef40757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559873 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(72),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{72}=0.25.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559875 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559875 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559875 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559875 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey asks a group of students about their eye color. The findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Green | Blue | Brown | Black | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $12$ | $8$ | $10$ | $40$ |\n| Boys | $13$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $60$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $24$ | $23$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nLet $B$ be the event that a randomly selected student is a **\"Boy\"**, and $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student has **\"Brown\"** eyes. Estimate $P(B\\cap C).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559875 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4bda52d-6631-58b0-bd68-5066bd3ff243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559875 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $B\\cap C$ is the event that a randomly selected student is a boy and has brown eyes. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $B\\cap C,$ we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Brown\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Brown\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Green | Blue | Brown | Black | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $12$ | $8$ | $10$ | $40$ |\n| Boys | $13$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $60$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $24$ | $23$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap C)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
